<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur Grimble</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Arthur Grimble</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur Grimble was a British colonial administrator who served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a> from 1914 and became its resident commissioner in 1926, later governing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a>. He recorded Gilbertese traditions and customs, and his humorous memoir A Pattern of Islands (1952) became one of the best-known accounts of life in a Pacific colony.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arthur Francis Grimble was born on 11 June 1888. He was educated at Chigwell School and Magdalene College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for postgraduate studies. On joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> in 1914 he became the very first cadet administrative officer in the Gilbert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From April 1919 Grimble acted as resident commissioner until Herbert Reginald McClure took up the post, and he later succeeded McClure. He learned the Gilbertese language and became a specialist in the myths and oral traditions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a> people, staying in the islands until 1933. He was then Administrator and Colonial Secretary of St Vincent from 1933, Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a> from 1936 to 1942 and Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a> from 1942 to 1948.</p><p>He was made a Companion of the Order of St Michael and St George in the 1930 New Year Honours and promoted to Knight Commander of the order in the 1938 New Year Honours. In 1944 he was appointed Honorary Colonel of the Windward Islands Battalion. In 1931 he designed the coat of arms of the colony of the Gilbert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a>, granted in 1937, and the design has been kept for the flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiribati">Kiribati</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>After retiring to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1948 Grimble became a writer and broadcaster, and his BBC radio broadcasts of the 1950s shaped many people's picture of the islands. A Pattern of Islands, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> by John Murray in 1952 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as We Chose the Islands, and its sequel Return to the Islands (1957) were both bestsellers. Pacific Destiny, a film based on his experiences, was released in 1956, the year of his death on 13 December. His scholarly work on Gilbertese culture is covered in Henry Evans Maude's book Tungaru Traditions: Writings on the Atoll Culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a> (1989).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arthur Grimble” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Island councils and colonial regulations replaced older authority, and <b>Arthur Grimble's</b> memoirs described the colony's administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/gilbert-and-ellice-islands">Gilbert and Ellice Islands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in British Hong Kong. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1932" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Arthur Grimble"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1932">What was here in 1932? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
